 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Victoria's job market has weakened for women over the past year. The figures below are the latest ABS data for September 2026, seasonally adjusted.\n\n**Women in Victoria, September 2026**\n- **Unemployment rate:** 5.1%. It was 4.1% in September 2025, so it is up about 1 percentage point. It peaked at 5.2% in July 2026 and has eased slightly since.\n- **Participation rate:** 63.6%, down from 63.9% a year earlier. It dropped to 62.4% in April 2026 and has mostly recovered.\n- **Employment-to-population ratio:** 60.4%, down from 61.3% a year earlier. Its low was 59.5% in April 2026.\n- **Employed women:** about 1.843 million, roughly flat on a year ago (up about 5,000). Employment fell to about 1.803 million in April and has since recovered.\n- **Full-time employment:** about 1.008 million, down about 2,000 on a year ago.\n\n**Comparisons**\n- **Victorian women and men:** Women's unemployment rate (5.1%) is now almost the same as men's (5.0%). A year ago women's was well below men's (4.1% versus 5.3%). This is because women's unemployment rose while men's eased. Men's participation is higher, at 71.5%.\n- **Victorian women and women nationally:** The national rate for women is 4.6%, up from 4.2% a year ago. Victorian women's unemployment is therefore higher, and it has risen faster.\n\n**Summary:** Employment has held up roughly level, and the recovery since April is a good sign. Unemployment is still about a point higher than a year ago. The ratio of women in work is also lower, because the working-age population has grown while the number of jobs hasn't. These monthly state estimates are volatile. Treat movements of a few tenths of a point as noise, and look at the trend over several months.",
